ePiX -- INSTALL

Last Change: January 11, 2005

This file contains detailed installation instructions for ePiX-1.x

Lines starting with a "%" denote commands typed at a shell prompt. 
Lines starting with a "#" denote commands run as root.
Don't type the prompt characters "%" or "#". :)


The following software must be installed on your system in order for
you to use ePiX:

  * The GNU shell, bash
  * A C++ compiler and development libraries, preferably g++
  * GhostScript, ImageMagick, and teTeX
  * GNU binutils, textutils, and fileutils (ar, sed, grep, ln, rm...)

(For the curious, the specific programs are: convert, dvips, g++,
latex, and ps2epsi.) Please consult your system documentation for
information on installing these components. The POST-INSTALL file has
potentially helpful information, as well.

ePiX comprises a header file (epix.h), a compiled library (libepix.a),
and four shell scripts (epix, elaps, flix, and laps) that automate
file processing by running auxiliary programs.  The shell scripts must
be in your PATH.


--- Synopsis ---

(1) Unpack
(2) Edit the config file if necessary
(3) Build:

% make [contrib] test 
% make install


--- Step-by-step Instructions ---

(1) Tarballs are available gzip or bzip2 compressed. GNU/Linux comes with
  bzip2 utilities, while some Unices may not. To unpack the tarball, use
  the appropriate version number, package name (src or complete), and
  tar options (z for .gz, j for .bz2, v to see filenames unpacked):

% tar -zxf epix-x.y.z_complete.tar.gz

% tar -jxf epix-x.y.z_complete.tar.bz2

  If your "tar" doesn't understand decompression, do

% gunzip -c epix-x.y.z_complete.tar.gz | tar -xf -

% bzcat epix-x.y.z_complete.tar.bz2 | tar -xf -

  Change to the source directory:

% cd epix-x.y.z


(2) By default, ePiX installs in /usr/local, and uses /bin/bash and
  g++ to compile files. If these assumptions are correct, please skip
  to step (3). Otherwise, edit the config file, changing the following
  lines as necessary:

  EPIX_ROOTDIR=/usr/local
  EPIX_CXX=g++
  EPIX_BASH_PATH=/bin/bash


(3) Compile and install:

% make

  This builds several object files, and the following files for
  installation:
  * A static library (libepix.a)
  * A header file (epix.h)
  * Four shell scripts

  Next, compile the documentation and sample files. These may be
  built/cleaned individually ("make doc", "make samples-clean", etc.)

% make test

  Compiling the figures will take a minute or two.
  
  Finally, if desired build the contributed package:

% make contrib

  These steps may be issued in a single command:

% make contrib test


  You must have write permission in the install directory you selected
  in step (2). If you have been logged on as yourself until now (a Very
  Good Idea:) and are installing in a system directory, do:

% su
Password: <your root password>

# make install

  (The root prompt on many systems is # rather than %.) If the install
  succeeds, you're done:

# exit

  If you are installing in your HOME directory, just type

% make install


  Installing and using multiple versions:

  More than one version of ePiX can be installed on a machine. To
  install a version older than 1.0.3 (in the example below, version
  0.8.9 is installed in the user's home directory), download and build
  the source code as usual; please visit

    http://savannah.nongnu.org/cgi-bin/viewcvs/epix/epix/archive/

  for source tarballs. Next, MANUALLY install the header and library,

# install -m 644 epix.h ~/include/epix-0.8.9.h
# install -m 644 libepix.a ~/lib/libepix-0.8.9.a

  The version number must be specified in the target names, and old
  shell scripts must NOT be installed. To use version 0.8.9, the
  source file must #include "epix-0.8.9.h", and is compiled with the
  epix flag "--no-defaults":

% epix --no-defaults -I ~/include -L ~/lib -lepix-0.8.9 file.c


(4) Post-Install:

  Documentation is installed in $INSTALL_DIR/share/doc/epix, as compiled
  files (ps, pdf) and tarred LaTeX sources.

  The directory $INSTALL_DIR/share/epix/config contains miscellaneous
  optional files, notably Jay Belanger's emacs mode for working with
  ePiX source files, and completions for users of Ian MacDonald's bash
  completions package. POST-INSTALL has instructions on installing and
  using these files.

  From the source directory, do
  
% make clean

  to clean the source tree of compiled files, documentation, and samples.
  It is safe to run "make clean" repeatedly.


(5) Uninstalling:

  To remove ePiX from your system, go to the directory where the
  sources were unpacked and do:

% make -n uninstall-all
       ^^----------------- *** N.B. ***

  The "-n" flag prints the commands that will be run, but does not
  remove anything.

  ***  SKIPPING THIS SANITY CHECK MAY DAMAGE YOUR FILESYSTEM!!  ***

  Verify carefully that the install path is correct, particularly that
  there are no extra spaces.  If the install path in make's output is
  correct, "su root" if necessary and do

# make uninstall-all

  ePiX also provides a "make uninstall" target, which removes only the
  version corresponding to the source directory. 


